Here's a complete example of an MCP server wrapping a REST API with stdio transport.

---

### **Tool Class — `ApiTools.cs`**

```csharp
using ModelContextProtocol.Server;
using System.ComponentModel;
using System.Text.Json;

[McpServerToolType]
public class ApiTools(HttpClient httpClient, ILogger<ApiTools> logger)
{
    [McpServerTool, Description("Get a user by their numeric ID.")]
    public async Task<string> GetUser(
        [Description("The numeric user ID to look up")] int userId,
        CancellationToken cancellationToken = default)
    {
        logger.LogInformation("Fetching user {UserId}", userId);
        var json = await httpClient.GetStringAsync(
            $"/users/{userId}", cancellationToken);
        return json;
    }

    [McpServerTool, Description("List all posts, optionally filtered by user ID.")]
    public async Task<string> ListPosts(
        [Description("Optional user ID to filter posts")] int? userId = null,
        CancellationToken cancellationToken = default)
    {
        var url = userId.HasValue ? $"/posts?userId={userId}" : "/posts";
        var json = await httpClient.GetStringAsync(url, cancellationToken);
        return json;
    }

    [McpServerTool(Destructive = false, ReadOnly = true),
     Description("Search users by name substring.")]
    public async Task<string> SearchUsers(
        [Description("Name substring to search for")] string query,
        CancellationToken cancellationToken = default)
    {
        var response = await httpClient.GetStringAsync("/users", cancellationToken);
        var users = JsonSerializer.Deserialize<JsonElement>(response);
        var matches = users.EnumerateArray()
            .Where(u => u.GetProperty("name").GetString()!
                .Contains(query, StringComparison.OrdinalIgnoreCase))
            .ToList();
        return JsonSerializer.Serialize(matches);
    }
}
```

### **`Program.cs` — stdio transport**

```csharp
using Microsoft.Extensions.DependencyInjection;
using Microsoft.Extensions.Hosting;
using Microsoft.Extensions.Logging;

var builder = Host.CreateApplicationBuilder(args);

// CRITICAL: Log to stderr only — stdout is the JSON-RPC transport
builder.Logging.AddConsole(options =>
    options.LogToStandardErrorThreshold = LogLevel.Trace);

// Register HttpClient with a base address for the API
builder.Services.AddHttpClient<ApiTools>(client =>
{
    client.BaseAddress = new Uri("https://jsonplaceholder.typicode.com");
});

builder.Services.AddMcpServer()
    .WithStdioServerTransport()
    .WithToolsFromAssembly();

await builder.Build().RunAsync();
```

---

### **How DI works for MCP tools**

The SDK supports two injection styles:

| Style | Class type | How services arrive |
|-------|-----------|---------------------|
| **Constructor injection** | Non-static class | Services in the constructor (e.g., `HttpClient`, `ILogger<T>`) |
| **Method parameter injection** | Static class | DI services as method params alongside tool params |

**Key rule:** The SDK auto-resolves any parameter whose type is registered in DI. These **never appear** in the tool's JSON schema sent to the LLM. Only parameters with `[Description]` are exposed as tool inputs.

Auto-injected types include:
- `CancellationToken` — cooperative cancellation
- `IMcpServer` — server instance for notifications
- `IProgress<ProgressNotificationValue>` — progress reporting
- **Any DI-registered service** — `HttpClient`, `ILogger<T>`, your own services

In the example above, `ApiTools` uses **constructor injection** via a primary constructor. The `AddHttpClient<ApiTools>()` call registers a typed `HttpClient` that the SDK injects when creating `ApiTools` instances. `ILogger<T>` is registered by default — no extra setup needed.
